  "subtitle": "the box that made the radio spectrum feel like a data network",
  "why_scout_cares": "A 21-LED dashboard on a metal box, sitting between your computer and two radios, translating the invisible radio spectrum into text on your screen. The PK-232 decoded packet radio, RTTY, Morse code, weather fax, and AMTOR — all from a single unit. The physical ritual: fire up a terminal program, type arcane commands, tune your radio until the green tuning-indicator LEDs lock on, and watch text or weather satellite images materialize out of static. This was the pre-web internet — a network made of radio waves and volunteer operators — and the PK-232 was the box that made it accessible. No other museum artifact bridges radio and computing in this way.",
